From 0b2212a906dfa31682a592f99d66abd48fbf14e1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ZhangDM-520 <jixianqiang1540@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 20:01:28 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat(animation): windows_drag physics tuning keys
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The drag physics feel was compile-time: [animation.windows_drag] exposed
only `physics`, and the elastic sheet always ran with the shipped
constants. Six keys now tune it (defaults = those constants, so behavior
is unchanged out of the box):

  stiffness          36.0    return strength of each sheet point
  damping             6.5    wobble decay
  coupling          100.0    rigidity between neighbouring points
  pointer_response    2.0    how far a motion loads the sheet (0 = rigid)
  max_displacement_px 200.0  excursion cap in logical pixels
  settle_after_pause_s 0.25  rendering pause before the sheet snaps to rest

Out-of-range values clamp to their range with the usual named diagnostic;
the keys apply from the next drag on (a drag already in flight keeps its
tuning). The lattice size, contraction factor, 240 Hz step and settle
thresholds stay fixed: the shader and the frame pacing own them.

Review round: the ranges bound termination — stiffness and damping floor
at 1.0 (zero of either lets a released drag deform or ring forever), and
a released sheet whose motion stays drained below the settle velocity is
snapped to rest after a quiet window, so no tuning can schedule frames
forever. settle_after_pause_s floors at 0.1 and its stall guard is
refresh-rate aware (the gap must dwarf the tick cadence as well as the
tuned pause), so no in-range value disables the physics on ordinary
frames at any refresh rate; the docs describe it as the frame-stall
guard it is.

CI: begin() loses its default argument for a shipping overload — stock
GCC rejects `Tuning tuning = {}` there, because a nested aggregate's
default member initializers are not available in a default argument
inside the enclosing class (only in function bodies).

Review round 3: the stall guard measured its pace wrong. m_tickGapS
(the previous tick's interval) both exempted the first tick — a stall
before any cadence was known integrated the whole gap at 240 Hz and left
the sheet parked mid-deformation — and could be corrupted by the wake
interval itself: the motion that initiates a sheet lands mid-frame, so
its first gap is a partial frame, and treating that as the cadence made
the ordinary next frame read as a stall and cleared the deformation
early. The guard now measures against the output's refresh period,
passed per tick (the compositor reads it from the output; unknown
cadence falls back to the tuned pause alone, from the very first tick),
and the catch-up is bounded to 1 s per tick so a tolerated pause cannot
burn the compositor thread. An audit of the same family found the
released-sheet quiet window crediting whole tick gaps as quiet from a
single end-of-tick velocity sample — a long frame ending slow mid-ring
snapped the deformation away; quiet time now accumulates per integration
step from the velocity actually carried through the motion.

Validated with four new cases pinned to the review reproducers
(a stall before the first tick settles; the 1 ms wake interval on a 5 Hz
output no longer clears the deformation on the ordinary 200 ms frame; a
4.5 s tolerated pause integrates a bounded prefix and stays active; a
ring's turning point no longer counts its whole gap as quiet), and each
fix is mutation-attributed: restoring the old guard reddens the three
guard cases, unbounded catch-up reddens the pause case, the old quiet
accounting reddens the ring case. Meson units 129/129; harness drag/
21/21 (drag/physics and drag/physics_edges included).

Co-authored-by: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
